{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"utopic-frenzied-albino","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## This is a chosen trade, not a regression to re-investigate\n\nChange 2684 (bug `ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` / `9313-1fac-9f32-4b07`) lowers `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT`\nfrom 120 s to 60 s and routes it through the whole-probe budget. A direct and **deliberate**\nconsequence is that under I/O load the filesystem walk will not finish, so\n`docker_storage_bytes`, `docker_storage_used_percent` and `docker_unaccounted_bytes` will\npublish **nothing** for those intervals.\n\nThis ticket exists so that is a recorded decision rather than a mystery. Somebody seeing\n`docker_storage_bytes` gaps next month should find this ticket, not start a fresh investigation\n— which is exactly the failure mode `9313` itself was: a day spent on \"sparse publishing\"\nbecause a truncated run and an absent value were indistinguishable.\n\n**Distinguishing signal:** a gap here with `docker_du_seconds` at or near 60 and `probe_ok` = 1\nis THIS, working as designed — the walk was attempted, bounded, and did not finish, and the rest\nof the probe completed normally. A gap with `probe_ok` absent or `probe_truncated` = 1 is a\ndifferent problem and belongs to `9313`'s revert conditions.\n\n## The measurement — the Docker root cannot be walked in this budget\n\nOn the production Gerrit host, 2026-09-05, load average 3.45 (the ordinary condition, not a\npathological one):\n\n    95.01s  rc=124  timeout 95  du -x --block-size=1 --max-depth=1 /var/lib/docker\n    95.01s  rc=124  timeout 95  du -x --block-size=1 --max-depth=1 /var/lib/docker\n   125.03s  rc=124  timeout 125 du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker\n   125.04s  rc=124  timeout 125 du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker/overlay2\n\n**A single walk did not complete at 95 s, and did not complete at 125 s.** Twice each. So this\nis not \"the old code walked twice, halve it and it fits\" — the tree is simply larger than the\nbudget under load. ~1.88M files, and `install-observability.sh` runs the unit at\n`IOSchedulingClass=idle` and `Nice=10`, so the walk is starved by design on a box whose job is\nserving Gerrit and which has documented IOPS-saturation history\n(`smudgeless-friendless-longspur`).\n\n**That is a capacity finding, not a defect.** `9313` is the defect (uncomposed ceilings, and a\nprobe SIGTERM-ed mid-run); this is the physics underneath it.\n\n## Why 60 s is nevertheless the right bound\n\nThe reading is **bimodal**, not slow-and-variable. When the cache is warm the walk completes in\nwell under a minute — journald breadcrumbs at 17:48, 17:58, 18:03, 18:09 and 18:42 on\n2026-09-05 all carry a `docker /var/lib/docker bytes=` value. Under load it does not complete\nat any bound tried.\n\nGiven two modes and nothing in between, a 120 s attempt that fails costs a further minute of a\n240 s budget over a 60 s attempt that fails, and returns the same nothing. 60 s captures the\nfast mode and fails fast in the stalled one. Raising it back would not recover the metric; it\nwould only re-create `9313`.\n\n## What is lost\n\n- `docker_unaccounted_bytes` — the highest-value metric here, and the one the 2026-09-02\n  incident exists to justify: overlay2 measured from the FILESYSTEM minus what\n  `docker system df` accounts for, i.e. the bytes no `docker prune` can reach. It needs the\n  filesystem half, so it is silent whenever the walk is.\n- `docker_storage_bytes` / `docker_storage_used_percent` — the cap-in-force readings.\n- Their alarms are `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, so **these gaps will page.** That is the\n  correct behaviour for a dead publisher and the wrong behaviour for a bounded, deliberate\n  non-measurement, and reconciling the two is part of this ticket's work rather than something\n  to paper over by weakening the alarms.\n\n## Options, none yet chosen — this needs measurement, not a preference\n\n1. **Walk less.** `overlay2/*/diff` is the bulk. A `--max-depth=2` census over `overlay2` alone,\n   or sampling layer directories, trades exactness for completion. Needs measuring: it may not\n   be meaningfully cheaper, since the cost is the stat walk, not the aggregation.\n2. **A cheaper estimator.** XFS/ext4 project quotas or `df` on a dedicated Docker volume give an\n   O(1) reading. ADR 0112 already moved gate scratch to its own volume for a related reason;\n   giving `/var/lib/docker` its own filesystem would make `docker_storage_bytes` a `df` read.\n   Probably the real answer, and the largest change.\n3. **Move the census out of band.** A separate low-frequency unit with a long timeout writes a\n   cache the probe reads, publishing `docker_census_age_seconds` so staleness is explicit. Keeps\n   the metric at reduced freshness instead of losing it.\n4. **Accept that it is unobtainable at this tree size** and retire the filesystem half, saying\n   so — which would also retire `docker_unaccounted_bytes`. The honest option, and the one to\n   beat.\n\nWhichever is chosen, the alarm treatment for a deliberate non-measurement must be settled with\nit.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The chosen option is measured against the alternatives on the production host, not\n      selected on plausibility — including the negative case, that walking less is not cheaper.\n- [ ] `docker_unaccounted_bytes` is either restored to continuous publication, or explicitly\n      retired with the incident rationale addressed.\n- [ ] The alarms stop paging for a bounded non-measurement while still paging for a dead\n      publisher — the two are distinguishable via `probe_ok` / `docker_du_seconds` as of 2684.\n- [ ] `docker_du_seconds` is used as the before/after evidence.\n- [ ] Portability: host shell + systemd + local validation; no dependence on a single CI provider.\n\n## Provenance\n\nMeasured 2026-09-05 while fixing `9313`. The 95 s and 125 s negative results are what stopped a\n\"halve the walk\" fix from shipping: removing the second traversal makes the probe survive, and\ndoes NOT make the reading obtainable. Recording the trade here so the degradation is a decision\nwith evidence attached rather than an undocumented behaviour change.\n","id":"5993-4cf7-0de9-4f72","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"docker_storage_bytes and docker_unaccounted_bytes will go intermittently blind under load — an ACCEPTED, measured degradation from bug 9313, because the Docker root cannot be walked inside the probe's budget"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788642709727512927,"uuid":"a1267032-c783-4608-9ed1-78ebb18d3eaf"}